{
  "scenario": {
    "id": "P01",
    "title": "Clean boot from reset to blink and UART",
    "layer": "2 Boot",
    "description": "The reference run. Power-on, the core reads the vector table (initial MSP and Reset_Handler), the startup code copies .data from flash to SRAM and zeroes .bss, then calls main. main prints \"boot ok\" over the UART, blinks the LED three times, increments a .bss counter and prints a .data value. Every boot step is a trace event, so this run is the baseline every other boot scenario is compared against.\n",
    "program": "blink",
    "options": {},
    "config": {
      "seed": 1,
      "max_cycles": 50000
    },
    "expected": {
      "summary": "All six boot steps pass, \"boot ok\", \"counter=1\" and \"greeting_len=7\" are printed, no faults, halt at the end of main.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "check": "boot_complete"
        },
        {
          "check": "no_faults"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_contains",
          "text": "boot ok"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_contains",
          "text": "counter=1"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_contains",
          "text": "greeting_len=7"
        },
        {
          "check": "min_events",
          "kind": "gpio",
          "count": 6
        },
        {
          "check": "event_sequence",
          "kinds": [
            "boot_step:power_on",
            "boot_step:vector_table",
            "boot_step:reset_handler",
            "boot_step:data_copied",
            "boot_step:bss_zeroed",
            "boot_step:main_entered",
            "uart_tx",
            "gpio",
            "halt"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "what_to_look_for": "The first six events are boot steps in the order power_on, vector_table, reset_handler, data_copied, bss_zeroed, main_entered. The vector_table event shows the two words the core read: MSP=0x20082000 (top of SRAM) and the Reset_Handler address in flash. The data_copied and bss_zeroed events report how many words were handled and that the check passed. main_entered gives the cycle count from reset to main, the number a student measures with the DWT counter on the board. After that: one UART line, six LED edges, two more UART lines, and a halt event.\n",
    "likely_root_causes": [
      "This is the healthy baseline: nothing is wrong. If your board does not show this sequence, compare its first divergence against the P02/P03 traces."
    ],
    "how_to_debug": [
      "On the board: set a hardware breakpoint at Reset_Handler, reset, and single-step; watch SP and PC in the registers view.",
      "In GDB: 'monitor reset halt' stops in the RP2350 boot ROM; 'hbreak Reset_Handler' and 'continue', then 'x/2xw 0x10000000': SP must equal word 0 and PC word 1 with bit 0 cleared, because the boot ROM loads them from your table.",
      "Count cycles from reset to main with DWT CYCCNT (enable TRCENA in DEMCR first) and compare with the main_entered event."
    ],
    "what_this_means": "Between power-on and main() the processor does only three things on its own: load MSP, load PC from the reset vector and start executing. Everything else (copying .data, zeroing .bss, calling main) is code the student wrote. The trace makes that split visible: the first two events are hardware, the rest is startup code.\n",
    "hidden_symptom": "LED blinks and the terminal shows three lines."
  },
